{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a movie that tells the story of the founding of facebook and the people behind it. the film is directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich. the movie follows mark zuckerberg, a harvard undergraduate, who creates a website called facemash that becomes a huge success. this leads to him being approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss to create a social networking site for harvard students.\n\nmark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, is portrayed as a complex character who is both brilliant and socially awkward. he is helped by his friend eduardo saverin, played by andrew garfield, who provides the initial funding for the site. as the site grows in popularity, mark and eduardo have different visions for the site, leading to a rift between them. meanwhile, the winklevoss brothers, played by armie hammer, claim that mark stole their idea and sue him.\n\nthe movie explores the themes of friendship, loyalty, and the consequences of success. it also raises questions about the nature of innovation and who deserves credit for it. the film is shot in a dark and moody style, which reflects the intense and often cutthroat world of silicon valley. the dialogue is quick and witty, with aaron sorkin's signature fast-paced repartee.\n\nthe performances in the movie are excellent, with jesse eisenberg standing out as mark zuckerberg. he brings a depth and nuance to the character, making him both relatable and unsympathetic at the same time. andrew garfield is also great as eduardo saverin, bringing a sense of vulnerability and tragedy to the character. the supporting cast, including justin timberlake as sean parker, is also excellent.\n\none of the most interesting aspects of the movie is its portrayal of the early days of facebook. it shows how the site 